// ceesafec 0.47.0: a static function of an `impl I for T` written in another package than `I`'s must be `public`, or
// generic code in `I`'s package that calls it through a bound does not check.
//
// `ceesafec fetch && ceesafec check` in this directory reports, at the call in `use`:
//
//   error[N0222]: 'iface.build' cannot be used with 'Thing': for these type arguments its body has an error:
//   'Thing.make' is not public: only its own package can call it (type-system.md §2) (type-system.md §20.4)
//
// (When the call is inside the dependency's own generic code, as json's `Decode.vector<T>` calling `T.fromJson`, it
// is instead N0364 at the manifest, "the dependency 'json' has errors", with the same note.)
//
// type-system.md §2 says: "A function of an `impl I for T` block takes no modifier but `public`, which has no effect:
// the method is as visible as the interface." Here `public` has an effect: writing `public fn make` makes it check.
// Methods with a receiver are not affected: `twice` without `public`, called through the bound in `doubled`, is
// accepted.
//
// Found with json's `FromJson.fromJson(value) -> Result<Self, DecodeError>`, a static function: every program's
// `impl FromJson for MyType` had to write `public fn fromJson`. Workaround: json's README and tests write `public`
// on that function (harmless where it is not needed).
//
// Suggested fix: when an instantiation calls `T.f()` for an interface's static function through a bound, check its
// visibility as the interface's (as §2 says, and as is already done for methods), not as the impl function's own.

import iface.Make;
import iface.build;
import iface.doubled;

struct Thing {
    n: u32;
}

impl Make for Thing {
    fn make(seed: u32) -> Thing => Thing { n: seed };

    fn twice(borrow this) -> u32 => this.n * 2;
}

public fn use() -> u32 {
    let t = build<Thing>(21);
    return doubled(t);
}
